<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:10.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">2. Although I don't see any security benefit, such a sign=
al *may* be convenient to help EAP-TLS implementations update their state m=
achines to support TLS 1.3. For example,
 if an EAP-TLS state machine previously used to assume that a &#8220;TLS co=
mplete&#8221; signal from its TLS library was sufficient to advance to a ne=
w state where it will no longer accept TLS payload might break when TLS 1.3=
 is used. Such a state machine would not necessarily
 know when to advance to this state with some similar sort of signal that t=
he server is done.<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:10.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">A different state machine which simply will always proces=
s TLS payload even after a &#8220;TLS complete&#8221; signal from its TLS l=
ibrary may not need any updates at all to work with
 TLS 1.3. I believe such a state machine would be able to handle a NewSessi=
onTicket after TLS completion without issues even without a signal.<o:p></o=
:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:10.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">Windows currently happens to fall into the first camp, wh=
ere a signal is convenient. It seems to me that using close_notify is more =
semantically correct, but has some tradeoffs.<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:10.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">A. In some cases, where the commitment message may be abl=
e to allow for a shorter handshake, using close_notify doesn&#8217;t allow =
the client to send an alert, which is a non-starter
 in my opinion.<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">B. If we settle for an extra round trip, we can use close=
_notify and make sure the client always has at least 1 chance to send an al=
ert.<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:10.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">This seems to me to be roughly where we started the discu=
ssion. Perhaps I have some of the theoretical details regarding the authent=
icated end signal incorrect as my perception
 is definitely colored by being mainly a client implementor. I happen to pr=
efer the draft-13 commitment message because it seems to allow one less rou=
nd trip in some cases. I&#8217;m happy to see discussion occurring either w=
ay and hope my perspective as an implementor
 is useful in driving toward a resolution.<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:10.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt">Jorge Vergara<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>
<p class=3D"MsoPlainText"><font size=3D"2" face=3D"Calibri"><span style=3D"=
font-size:11.0pt"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span></font></p>
<div style=3D"border:none;border-top:solid #E1E1E1 1.0pt;padding:3.0pt 0in =
0in 0in">
